---
export const prerender = true;
import AppLayout from '../../layouts/AppLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';

const siteURL = Astro.site?.toString().replace(/\/$/, '') ?? 'https://pixelatmos.com';
const title = 'SVG vs PNG: Which Should You Use?';
const description =
  'The decision comes down to one question — is the image made of shapes or of pixels? What each format costs, where each breaks down, and why the answer is rarely both.';
const published = '2026-07-15';
const url = `${siteURL}/blog/svg-vs-png/`;

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BlogPosting',
  headline: title,
  description,
  datePublished: published,
  dateModified: published,
  inLanguage: 'en',
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'FilipaoVfx', url: 'https://github.com/FilipaoVfx' },
  publisher: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'Pixelatmos',
    logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: `${siteURL}/og-image.webp` },
  },
  mainEntityOfPage: { '@type': 'WebPage', '@id': url },
};
---

<AppLayout title={`${title} — Pixelatmos`} description={description}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ name: 'Blog', href: '/blog/' }, { name: 'SVG vs PNG' }]} />
  </div>

  <article
    class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 pb-12 prose prose-invert prose-lg prose-headings:font-brutal prose-headings:uppercase prose-a:text-brutal-neon-green text-gray-300"
  >
    <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-6xl font-black mb-4 !text-brutal-neon-green">
      SVG vs PNG: Which Should You Use?
    </h1>
    <div class="not-prose font-mono text-sm text-gray-400 mb-12 border-b border-gray-800 pb-4">
      By <a href="/about/" class="text-brutal-neon-cyan">FilipaoVfx</a> · Published July 15, 2026 · ~5 min read
    </div>

    <p class="lead">
      Use SVG when the image is made of shapes — logos, icons, illustrations, charts. Use PNG when it
      is made of pixels — screenshots, photographs, anything with a texture that was captured rather
      than drawn. Almost every real decision follows from that one distinction, and the cases where
      it is genuinely close are rarer than the debate suggests.
    </p>

    <h2>They Store Different Things</h2>
    <p>
      A PNG is a grid of pixel values. A 512×512 icon is 262,144 colour samples, whatever it depicts.
      An SVG is a set of instructions: <em>draw a circle here, fill it with this gradient, cut this
      path out of it</em>. The browser executes those instructions at whatever size the layout needs.
    </p>
    <p>
      Everything else is downstream of that. SVG scales without blurring because it re-executes at
      the new size. PNG blurs because there is nothing to re-execute — only samples to interpolate.
      SVG can be styled by CSS and animated per element because its shapes are DOM nodes. PNG cannot,
      because it has no parts.
    </p>

    <h2>Where Each One Wins</h2>
    <div class="not-prose my-8 overflow-x-auto border-2 border-black shadow-brutal-sm">
      <table class="w-full border-collapse bg-brutal-dark-bg font-mono text-[12px]">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b-2 border-black bg-black/40 text-left">
            <th class="px-4 py-2.5 font-brutal text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-brutal-neon-green">Use case</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-2.5 font-brutal text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-brutal-neon-green">Format</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-2.5 font-brutal text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-brutal-neon-green">Why</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-gray-400">
          <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-bold text-white">Logo</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-brutal-neon-green">SVG</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">One file for a favicon and a billboard.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-bold text-white">UI icons</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-brutal-neon-green">SVG</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">Recolour with CSS; crisp on every display density.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-bold text-white">Charts</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-brutal-neon-green">SVG</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">Text stays selectable and searchable.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-bold text-white">Photographs</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-brutal-neon-cyan">WebP / AVIF</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">Lossy compression built for continuous tone.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-bold text-white">Screenshots</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-brutal-neon-cyan">PNG</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">Lossless — text stays legible, no artefacts.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-bold text-white">Complex illustration</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-brutal-neon-cyan">PNG</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">Thousands of paths outweigh a raster of the same thing.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>The Size Question Is Not What People Think</h2>
    <p>
      "SVG is smaller" holds for simple graphics and inverts for complex ones. A three-shape icon is
      a few hundred bytes of SVG against several kilobytes of PNG. A detailed illustration with
      thousands of paths, gradients and filters can easily exceed a well-compressed raster of the
      same artwork.
    </p>
    <p>
      The crossover is roughly where you stop being able to name the shapes. If you can describe the
      graphic as "a circle, two rounded rectangles and a path", SVG wins comfortably. If it is a
      hand-drawn scene with texture, render it once and ship pixels.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two things distort the comparison. SVG is text, so it gzips well — the transferred size is
      often half the file size, which raw file-size comparisons miss. And SVG exports carry
      significant dead weight; see <a href="/blog/how-to-optimize-svg-files/">how to optimize SVG
      files</a> for what is safe to strip, or run one through the
      <a href="/svg-optimizer/">SVG Optimizer</a> and compare.
    </p>

    <h2>Where SVG Breaks Down</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Fonts.</strong> An SVG referencing a font renders in whatever the viewer has. Convert
      text to paths before shipping, or accept the substitution.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Filters.</strong> Blurs and drop shadows are computed at render time. A page with many
      filtered SVGs can cost more CPU than the equivalent raster.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Untrusted files.</strong> SVG is XML that can carry scripts and event handlers. If you
      accept user-uploaded SVG, sanitize it before rendering — a PNG has no such attack surface.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Email.</strong> SVG support in email clients remains poor. Use PNG.
    </p>

    <h2>What About WebP and AVIF?</h2>
    <p>
      For photographic content they beat PNG substantially — often 25–50% smaller at equivalent
      quality — and both support transparency. The honest modern advice is: SVG for shapes, WebP or
      AVIF for photographs, PNG when you specifically need lossless raster such as a screenshot with
      fine text.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is one reason the wallpapers here are served as <a href="/wallpapers/">WebP</a>: they are
      photographic, so a vector format would make no sense.
    </p>

    <h2>The Capability Nobody Weighs</h2>
    <p>
      SVG stays editable after export. A logo shipped as SVG can be recoloured for a dark theme,
      animated stroke by stroke, or fed to the <a href="/3d-lab/svg-to-3d/">SVG to 3D converter</a>,
      which turns each shape into its own 3D layer with its own depth and material.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same logo as a PNG is a finished picture. That difference rarely appears in a
      format-comparison table, and it is often the one that matters six months later.
    </p>

    <div class="not-prose mt-12 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3">
      <a href="/svg-optimizer/" class="group border-2 border-black bg-brutal-dark-bg p-4 shadow-brutal-sm transition-transform hover:-translate-y-0.5">
        <h3 class="font-brutal text-sm font-black uppercase tracking-wide text-white group-hover:text-brutal-neon-green">SVG Optimizer</h3>
        <p class="mt-1 font-mono text-[11px] leading-relaxed text-gray-500">See what your file is carrying.</p>
      </a>
      <a href="/blog/how-to-optimize-svg-files/" class="group border-2 border-black bg-brutal-dark-bg p-4 shadow-brutal-sm transition-transform hover:-translate-y-0.5">
        <h3 class="font-brutal text-sm font-black uppercase tracking-wide text-white group-hover:text-brutal-neon-green">Optimizing SVG</h3>
        <p class="mt-1 font-mono text-[11px] leading-relaxed text-gray-500">What is safe to strip, what breaks.</p>
      </a>
      <a href="/3d-lab/svg-to-3d/" class="group border-2 border-black bg-brutal-dark-bg p-4 shadow-brutal-sm transition-transform hover:-translate-y-0.5">
        <h3 class="font-brutal text-sm font-black uppercase tracking-wide text-white group-hover:text-brutal-neon-cyan">SVG to 3D</h3>
        <p class="mt-1 font-mono text-[11px] leading-relaxed text-gray-500">The thing PNG can never do.</p>
      </a>
    </div>
  </article>
</AppLayout>
